From daniel.e.ho-Re5JQEeQqe8AvxtiuMwx3w at public.gmane.org  Wed Oct 10 15:58:55 2007
From: daniel.e.ho-Re5JQEeQqe8AvxtiuMwx3w at public.gmane.org (Daniel Ho)
Date: Wed, 10 Oct 2007 08:58:55 -0700
Subject: Re: befuddled by match.matrix
In-Reply-To: <470CD368.7050609-gpQNmkgQrW4@public.gmane.org>
References: <Pine.LNX.4.64.0709302021490.7464@ls01.fas.harvard.edu> <470CD368.7050609@unc.edu>
Message-ID: <31b03d500710100858rd1226b7s498206f31876cd5a@mail.gmail.com>

Your original data frame has unique row names corresponding to each of
the observations, which are used in match.matrix.  Depending on the
format of your input data these may or may not be numbers.  The row
names of match.matrix correspond to the names of the treated, and each
of the cells corresponds to a name of matched controls.

So for example:

> data(lalonde)
> row.names(lalonde)

  [1] "NSW1"    "NSW2"   . . .  "NSW185"   "PSID1"   "PSID2"  . . . "PSID429"

> foo <- matchit(treat ~ re74 + re75 + educ, data=lalonde)
> data.frame(foo$match.matrix)
            X1
NSW1   PSID352
NSW2   PSID271
NSW3   PSID418
NSW4   PSID382
NSW5   PSID355
...

Dan

